@meta
id: 08-06
title: Take the whole line
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d
par: 13
minutes: 3
vscode: click the line, Home, Shift-Down, Delete
open: order.provider.test.ts

@brief
Deleting a line in VS Code is a small ceremony: click somewhere on it,
make sure the selection covers the newline as well, delete, then check
you have not left a blank line behind.

In vim it is one key pressed twice, from anywhere on the line. The column
does not matter. Nothing has to be selected first.

This test has a stale TODO comment on it and a debug log that was never
meant to be committed. Both lines go. Nothing else in the file changes.

@keys
| `dd` | delete the whole line the cursor is on |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
The verb for delete is `d`, and on its own it waits for you to say how
much. Doubling it means "this line".
--
`dd` deletes the line under the cursor, newline and all, and lands you on
the line that takes its place.
--
`j` then `dd` clears the comment. You are now on the `it(` line, so `j`
again and `dd` clears the log.

@solution
    j dd  j dd  :wq<Enter>

Six keystrokes. The detail to notice is where the cursor ends up: after
`dd` it is on the line that moved up into the gap, in the same column.
That is why deleting several separate lines is `dd`, move, `dd` — you
never have to re-establish where you are.

Doubling is how every operator in vim says "this whole line". `dd`, `cc`,
`yy`, `>>`, `==`. Learn the pattern once and the rest of them come free.

And `d` on its own is not a command. It is a verb waiting for an object.
Chapter 9 is entirely about what you can put after it.

